  "title": "Agent code review: 7 traps that slip through",
  "content": "Most frontend performance guides assume a clean environment and a patient timeline. The default configuration is fine right up until it isn't. This is the version of the write-up that includes the part that broke.\n\nAgent-generated code arrives with a confidence that human code rarely has. It compiles, it passes the tests the agent wrote for itself, and it reads like it was written by someone who has read every style guide. The problem is that the agent has also read every outdated tutorial, every Stack Overflow answer from 2014, and every pattern that was correct until a library changed its API two major versions ago. At team scale, the review process that catches human mistakes — the one built around \"does this person understand what they're doing?\" — quietly stops working, because the agent always sounds like it understands.\n\nWhat follows are seven traps that show up repeatedly when teams review agent output, framed as a listicle because each one is a distinct pattern with a distinct fix. Most of them are not new mistakes. They are old mistakes wearing a new author's name, which is exactly why they slip through: the reviewer recognizes the shape [of the code](/test-ai-code-without-testing-the-ai/) as familiar and stops looking at the details.\n\n## What this list actually solves\n\nThe core problem is that agent output changes the economics [of code review.](/7-code-review-traps-when-ai-writes-half-your-code/) A human contributor who writes a `setTimeout`-based retry loop usually has a reason, even if it's a bad one, and a reviewer can ask about it. An agent that writes the same loop is pattern-matching from training data, and the reviewer's usual signal — \"this person seems to know what they're doing\" — is now actively misleading. The agent always seems to know what it's doing. That's the whole point of it.\n\nSo the review process has to shift from judging intent to verifying behavior. Each trap below is a place where the code looks intentional but the behavior is wrong, and each comes with a concrete check a reviewer can run.\n\n## Evaluation criteria\n\nBefore the list, a quick note on what makes a trap worth listing. A trap qualifies if it meets three conditions:\n\n1. **It passes the agent's own tests.** If the agent wrote a test that would catch the mistake, it usually does — the failures are in the seams between tests.\n2. **It looks correct to a reviewer skimming for intent.** The code reads as deliberate, not sloppy.\n3. **It fails in a way that is expensive to discover late.** Usually at integration, under load, or after a dependency upgrade.\n\nThat filter removes a lot of common complaints (formatting, naming, comment density) and leaves the ones that actually cost teams time.\n\n## 1. The retry loop with no jitter and no cap\n\n**What it does:** The agent generates a retry wrapper around an HTTP call or a database operation. It looks like this:\n\n```python\nimport time\nimport requests\n\ndef fetch_with_retry(url, max_retries=5):\n    for attempt in range(max_retries):\n        try:\n            return requests.get(url, timeout=10)\n        except requests.RequestException:\n            time.sleep(2 ** attempt)\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"failed after {max_retries} attempts\")\n```\n\n**Strength:** Exponential backoff is the right idea, and the agent gets the shape right almost every time. This is genuinely better than the naive `while True: try: ... except: pass` that shows up in older code.\n\n**Weakness:** There is no jitter, so every client that hits the same failing endpoint retries in lockstep. There is no cap on the backoff, so attempt 5 sleeps 16 seconds and attempt 10 would sleep over eight minutes if `max_retries` were raised. And there is no distinction between retryable and non-retryable errors — a 400 gets retried the same as a 503. The agent learned the pattern from tutorials that predate the widespread adoption of jittered backoff, and it reproduces the pattern faithfully.\n\n**Best for:** This is the single most common trap in the list. Every agent-generated retry loop should be reviewed against three questions: is there jitter, is there a cap, and does it distinguish error classes? AWS's own documentation on exponential backoff and jitter describes the full pattern; the agent usually implements the first third of it.\n\n## 2. The test that asserts the implementation, not the behavior\n\n**What it does:** The agent writes tests alongside the code, which is good. But the tests it writes tend to assert on internal calls rather than observable outcomes:\n\n```python\nfrom unittest.mock import patch, MagicMock\n\ndef test_process_order():\n    with patch(\"orders.db.save\") as mock_save:\n        process_order({\"id\": 1, \"total\": 100})\n        mock_save.assert_called_once_with({\"id\": 1, \"total\": 100, \"status\": \"pending\"})\n```\n\n**Strength:** The test exists, it runs, and it exercises the code path. Coverage tools will report it as covered.\n\n**Weakness:** The test is coupled to the exact call signature of an internal function. The next agent that refactors `save` to take a model object instead of a dict will break this test, and the fix will be to update the mock — which means the test now verifies nothing about behavior. Worse, the test passes even if `save` silently drops the data, because the mock never actually saves. The agent has produced the appearance of testing without the substance.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that use agents heavily for test generation should add a review rule: every mock assertion must be paired with at least one assertion on a real observable outcome — a return value, a database row, an HTTP response. If the test can't express that, the code under test is probably too coupled to its collaborators.\n\n## 3. The dependency pinned to a version that no longer exists\n\n**What it does:** The agent adds a dependency to `package.json`, `requirements.txt`, or `go.mod`, and the version it picks is whatever was current in its training data. Sometimes that version has been yanked. Sometimes the API has changed. Sometimes the package has been renamed.\n\n**Strength:** The dependency is usually real and the version is usually valid at the time it was written into the model's weights. The agent is not hallucinating a package from nothing — it's reproducing a real historical state.\n\n**Weakness:** The lockfile resolves, the install succeeds, and then a transitive dependency has a CVE that was patched two minor versions later. Or the agent uses an API that was removed in a major version bump, and the code fails at runtime with a `TypeError` that the test suite doesn't catch because the test suite was written against the same stale API. This is the trap where the outdated-tutorial origin is most visible: the agent is writing code for a version of the library that no longer exists.\n\n**Best for:** Any team using automated dependency updates. The fix is not to ban agent-added dependencies but to require that every new dependency in an agent PR comes with a lockfile diff and a note on which version the agent targeted. If the agent targeted a version more than one major behind current, treat the PR as needing a manual upgrade pass before merge.\n\n## 4. The async function that is secretly synchronous\n\n**What it does:** The agent writes `async def` functions that call blocking I/O inside them — `requests.get`, `time.sleep`, a synchronous database driver — under the assumption that `async` makes code concurrent.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport requests\n\nasync def fetch_all(urls):\n    results = []\n    for url in urls:\n        results.append(requests.get(url, timeout=10).text)\n    return results\n```\n\n**Strength:** The function signature is correct, the call site is correct, and the code runs. In a single-request test it behaves exactly as expected.\n\n**Weakness:** The event loop is blocked for the entire duration of every request. Under concurrency, this is worse than synchronous code, because the caller believes it has concurrency and allocates resources accordingly. The failure mode is a p99 latency spike that looks like a downstream problem until someone profiles the event loop. The agent reproduces this pattern because a huge amount of tutorial code uses `requests` inside `async def` without explanation.\n\n**Best for:** Teams running async web frameworks where a single blocking call can stall the whole worker. The review check is mechanical: search the diff for `requests.`, `time.sleep`, and synchronous database drivers inside any `async def`, and require an async-native equivalent (`httpx.AsyncClient`, `asyncio.sleep`, an async driver).\n\n## 5. The error handler that swallows the stack trace\n\n**What it does:** The agent wraps a block in `try/except` and logs a message without the exception object, or catches a broad exception class and continues.\n\n```python\ntry:\n    result = risky_operation()\nexcept Exception as e:\n    logger.error(f\"operation failed: {e}\")\n    return None\n```\n\n**Strength:** The error is handled, the process doesn't crash, and there is a log line. For a prototype, this is reasonable.\n\n**Weakness:** `str(e)` for many exception types is empty or uninformative. The stack trace — the part that tells you where the failure originated — is discarded. `except Exception` catches `KeyboardInterrupt`-adjacent failures and programming errors alike, so a `TypeError` from a typo gets logged as \"operation failed\" and the function returns `None`, which then propagates as a silent data loss. The agent learned this pattern from tutorials that prioritized \"don't crash\" over \"fail loudly.\"\n\n**Best for:** Any code path where a silent `None` can propagate. The review rule: every `except` block must either re-raise, log with `logger.exception(...)` (which captures the traceback), or return a typed error that the caller is forced to handle. A bare `return None` in an exception handler is a red flag in review.\n\n## 6. The workflow that assumes idempotency it doesn't have\n\n**What it does:** The agent generates a multi-step workflow — charge a card, send an email, update a record — and wires it together without considering what happens when step 2 fails after step 1 succeeded. Retries at the workflow level then double-charge or double-send.\n\n**Strength:** The happy path is correct and readable. Each step is individually well-written.\n\n**Weakness:** The workflow has no idempotency key, no compensation step, and no dead-letter path. When the retry logic from trap #1 kicks in, the first step runs again. This is the trap that is most expensive to discover in production, because the failure is a business-logic failure (a duplicate charge) rather than a technical one.\n\n**Best for:** Teams building agent-generated workflows that touch payments, notifications, or any external side effect. The review check: for every step that causes an external side effect, ask what happens if the step before it retries. If the answer is \"it runs twice,\" the workflow needs an idempotency key or an outbox pattern before merge.\n\n## 7. The configuration that hardcodes a value the environment should provide\n\n**What it does:** The agent writes a connection string, a timeout, a feature flag, or a model name directly into the code, often with a comment like `# adjust as needed`.\n\n**Strength:** The code runs immediately in the environment the agent was targeting. No setup required.\n\n**Weakness:** The value is now wrong in every other environment. Worse, if the value is a credential or an endpoint, it may be a real one from a tutorial that the agent reproduced verbatim. The `# adjust as needed` comment is the tell: the agent knows the value is environment-specific but has no mechanism to express that. This is the oldest trap on the list, and it persists because it's the one that most reliably produces a working demo.\n\n**Best for:** Every team, but especially teams deploying agent-generated code to multiple environments. The review check is a grep for hardcoded URLs, ports, model names, and timeout literals in the diff. Each one should be a named constant sourced from environment configuration, with a documented default.\n\n## The strongest default, and why\n\nIf a team can only adopt one practice from this list, it should be the **behavioral review checklist** — a short, mechanical set of greps and questions applied to every agent PR before a human reads it for intent. The reason is that six of the seven traps above are detectable without understanding the code's purpose. You don't need to know what the service does to notice a missing jitter, a `requests.get` inside `async def`, or a bare `return None` in an `except` block.\n\nA minimal version of that checklist, runnable as a script or a CI step:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# agent-review-checks.sh — run on the diff before human review\nset -euo pipefail\n\ndiff=$(git diff origin/main...HEAD)\n\n# Trap 4: blocking calls inside async functions\necho \"$diff\" | grep -nE 'async def' -A 20 | grep -E 'requests\\.|time\\.sleep' && \\\n  echo \"WARN: blocking call inside async function\" || true\n\n# Trap 5: swallowed exceptions\necho \"$diff\" | grep -nE 'except Exception' -A 3 | grep -E 'return None' && \\\n  echo \"WARN: exception handler returns None\" || true\n\n# Trap 7: hardcoded endpoints and credentials\necho \"$diff\" | grep -nE 'https?://[^\\\"]+' | grep -vE 'example\\.com|localhost' && \\\n  echo \"WARN: hardcoded URL in diff\" || true\n```\n\nThis is illustrative, not exhaustive — it catches three of the seven traps and produces false positives. But it shifts the review conversation from \"does this look right?\" to \"why did the check fire?\", which is a much better use of a senior reviewer's time.\n\n## Honorable mentions worth knowing about\n\nA few more patterns that don't quite make the top seven but show up often enough to recognize:\n\n- **The `datetime.utcnow()` call.** Deprecated in modern Python in favor of timezone-aware `datetime.now(timezone.utc)`, but present in a huge amount of tutorial code. The agent reproduces it faithfully. The failure is subtle: naive datetimes compared against aware ones raise `TypeError`, usually in a test that wasn't run.\n- **The `SELECT *` in an ORM query.** Fine for a demo, expensive when a column is added to a wide table. Agents generate it because it's shorter and reads as simpler.\n- **The `console.log` left in a production path.** Not a correctness bug, but a signal that the agent was iterating without a debugger and the reviewer should check what else was left behind.\n\n## Options that look appealing but fail in practice\n\nTwo approaches to agent code review are popular and don't hold up at team scale:\n\n**\"Let the agent review the agent.\"** Running a second model over the first model's output catches some surface issues but shares the same training-data biases. If the first agent learned the un-jittered retry pattern, the second agent is likely to consider it correct. It's useful as a first pass for style and obvious bugs, not as a substitute for the mechanical checks above.\n\n**\"Require 100% test coverage for agent PRs.\"** Coverage measures which lines executed, not which behaviors were verified. Trap #2 — the mock-heavy test — produces high coverage and verifies almost nothing. A coverage gate on agent PRs tends to produce more tests of the same shape, not better ones.\n\n## How to choose based on your situation\n\n| Situation | First practice to adopt |\n|---|---|\n| Small team, agent PRs are a minority | The mechanical grep checklist in CI |\n| Agent PRs are the majority of changes | Behavioral review checklist plus a rule that every mock assertion has a real-outcome counterpart |\n| Workflows touch external side effects | Idempotency review before merge, no exceptions |\n| Multiple deployment environments | Hardcoded-value grep plus a config-schema test |\n| Heavy reliance on generated tests | Replace coverage gates with mutation testing on critical paths |\n\nThe common thread: pick the practice that matches the failure mode you're most likely to hit, not the one that's easiest to automate. The mechanical checks are cheap and catch the most traps; the behavioral rules are more expensive but catch the ones that cause incidents.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How do I review agent-generated code without reading every line?**\n\nYou can't skip reading, but you can front-load the mechanical checks so the reading is focused. Run the grep-based checks from this post on the diff first, resolve the warnings, and then read the code for intent. The mechanical pass typically eliminates the low-value review comments (missing jitter, hardcoded URLs) and leaves the reviewer free to think about whether the workflow is correct.\n\n**Why does agent code pass tests but fail in production?**\n\nThe tests were written by the same agent that wrote the code, against the same stale assumptions. If the agent believes `requests.get` inside `async def` is fine, it will write a test that calls the function once and asserts the result — which passes. The failure only appears under concurrency, which the test never exercises. The fix is to require at least one test per agent PR that exercises the code under the conditions it will actually face: concurrent calls, retries, or a failing dependency.\n\n**What is the most common mistake in agent-generated retry logic?**\n\nMissing jitter. Exponential backoff without jitter causes synchronized retries across clients, which turns a transient downstream failure into a thundering herd. The second most common is retrying non-retryable errors — a 400 or a validation failure gets the same treatment as a 503. Both are correct in the tutorial the agent learned from and wrong in production.\n\n**Should agent-generated code be held to a different review standard than human code?**\n\nYes, but not a higher one — a more mechanical one. Human code carries intent that a reviewer can interrogate. Agent code carries pattern-matching that a reviewer can't interrogate, because the agent will confidently explain a wrong choice. The standard should emphasize verifiable behavior over apparent intent: does the retry have jitter, does the exception handler preserve the traceback, does the workflow tolerate a retry. Those questions have objective answers, which is exactly what you want when the author's confidence is not a signal.\n\n## Final recommendation\n\nStart with the mechanical checks. Open your CI configuration — whichever file defines your pipeline (`.github/workflows/`, `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `Jenkinsfile`) — and add a step that runs the grep checks from this post against the diff of any PR labeled as agent-generated. It will produce false positives and miss traps, and that's fine: the goal is to move the first pass of review from a human reading for intent to a script checking for behavior. Once that step is in place, the human review can focus on the two traps that no grep can catch — the mock-heavy test and the non-idempotent workflow — because those are the ones that cost the most to discover late.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
